WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.354 --> 00:00:02.285
- He's an English singer songwriter

00:00:02.285 --> 00:00:03.749
and occasional actor.

00:00:03.749 --> 00:00:07.400
In 2014, he was nominated
for best new artist

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.943
at the 56th annual Grammy awards.

00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:13.826
He received the songwriter
of the year award in 2015.

00:00:13.826 --> 00:00:15.848
He's Ed Sheeran, and here are his top ten

00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:17.387
rules for success.

00:00:17.387 --> 00:00:20.246
(whoosh)

00:00:20.246 --> 00:00:21.973
- Carrying on the momentum,

00:00:21.973 --> 00:00:23.995
I think that's the biggest
challenge for any artist.

00:00:23.995 --> 00:00:28.117
To get up to a stadium
level for any artist

00:00:28.117 --> 00:00:29.616
is kind of the peak,

00:00:29.616 --> 00:00:32.126
but then the challenge from
there is staying at that level.

00:00:32.126 --> 00:00:34.850
You know, to remain at the stadium level,

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.646
to keep releasing records that,

00:00:37.646 --> 00:00:39.354
not necessarily are hits,

00:00:39.354 --> 00:00:40.641
but just connects with people.

00:00:40.641 --> 00:00:43.811
I've never been worried
about my records selling

00:00:43.811 --> 00:00:45.453
millions of copies, but I have been

00:00:45.453 --> 00:00:49.244
really intent on writing
songs that come from me

00:00:49.244 --> 00:00:51.417
but when they go out
they belong to people.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:55.084
That people can relate
to them and get into it.

00:00:55.084 --> 00:00:56.922
And then, they'll come to the shows.

00:00:56.922 --> 00:00:58.097
The show is always the end goal,

00:00:58.097 --> 00:01:01.717
I see an album as a commercial to a show.

00:01:01.717 --> 00:01:02.550
- [Charlie] Yeah

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.346
- Buy the album, or download the album,

00:01:04.346 --> 00:01:06.221
or stream the album, or however
you listen to the album,

00:01:06.221 --> 00:01:08.047
and then you think I want
to go and see him live.

00:01:08.047 --> 00:01:09.515
And then you go to the show.

00:01:09.515 --> 00:01:11.409
I guess my advice to young performers is

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:12.467
just do you .

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:15.285
There's seven billion
people in this world,

00:01:15.285 --> 00:01:17.579
and there is no one like you

00:01:17.579 --> 00:01:19.172
and there is no one who is
going to write songs like you,

00:01:19.172 --> 00:01:20.365
and no one who's going to sing like you,

00:01:20.365 --> 00:01:22.483
as long as you keep it exactly yourself.

00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:24.053
I mean, imitate people to a point,

00:01:24.053 --> 00:01:26.469
to get your influence and kind of

00:01:26.469 --> 00:01:28.254
help you write songs and help you learn,

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:29.307
and someone to help you sing.

00:01:29.307 --> 00:01:30.334
But once you found your
voice, just do that,

00:01:30.334 --> 00:01:32.463
and stick with it.

00:01:32.463 --> 00:01:34.494
Even if it sounds like it's odd,

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:36.416
and people are telling you it won't work,

00:01:36.416 --> 00:01:38.418
just stick with it,
and eventually it will.

00:01:38.418 --> 00:01:39.910
'Cuz there's seven billion
people in the world,

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:42.131
there's bound to be one
person that likes your music.

00:01:42.131 --> 00:01:44.581
Have you ever heard of this
about the 10,000 hours theory?

00:01:44.581 --> 00:01:46.085
So that theory works.

00:01:46.085 --> 00:01:50.252
When I first started out, I
saw Damien Rice in concert.

00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:54.173
I mention it in a song, I said,

00:01:54.173 --> 00:01:55.656
I won't stop until my name is in lights

00:01:55.656 --> 00:01:57.211
stadium height with Damien Rice.

00:01:57.211 --> 00:01:58.743
And literally, like, I watched him

00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:00.548
and was like I want to do that one day,

00:02:00.548 --> 00:02:03.663
and wrote million songs did a million gigs

00:02:03.663 --> 00:02:06.872
and ended up kind of
doing a similar thing.

00:02:06.872 --> 00:02:09.698
But it literally came
from writing a song a day,

00:02:09.698 --> 00:02:11.108
or two songs a day, or five songs a day,

00:02:11.108 --> 00:02:12.922
and just getting all
these songs out of me.

00:02:12.922 --> 00:02:15.239
Doing a million gigs,
sometimes three gigs a day.

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:17.566
We did six gigs a day one in Glastonberry.

00:02:17.566 --> 00:02:20.107
I think, you view it as a dirt,

00:02:20.107 --> 00:02:21.474
when you switch dirt you tap on it,

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:23.886
it's going to flow sh-(bleep) water out

00:02:23.886 --> 00:02:25.614
for a substantial amount of time,

00:02:25.614 --> 00:02:27.213
and then clean water is
going to start flowing.

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:28.816
Now and then you'll get a bit of (bleep)

00:02:28.816 --> 00:02:30.270
but as long as it gets
out of you it's fine.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:31.103
(audience laughs)

00:02:31.103 --> 00:02:32.786
So with songs, you're going
to write sh-(bleep) songs

00:02:32.786 --> 00:02:34.035
at the beginning, you are.

00:02:34.035 --> 00:02:34.868
My songs were terrible,
my raps were terrible.

00:02:34.868 --> 00:02:36.821
I listened to it the
other day, it was awful.

00:02:36.821 --> 00:02:38.114
But I got it out of me,

00:02:38.114 --> 00:02:39.211
and the more and more you write,

00:02:39.211 --> 00:02:40.456
the more and more you experience.

00:02:40.456 --> 00:02:42.346
Then you start flowing clean water,

00:02:42.346 --> 00:02:44.231
and songs start getting
better and better and better.

00:02:44.231 --> 00:02:46.524
When you just, you're on a good streak,

00:02:46.524 --> 00:02:47.357
you're writing good songs,

00:02:47.357 --> 00:02:48.576
now and then you're going
to write a (bleep) song,

00:02:48.576 --> 00:02:51.646
and that song is out of
you and you can move on.

00:02:51.646 --> 00:02:52.625
It's the same with gigs.

00:02:52.625 --> 00:02:55.178
You will always play bad
gigs at the beginning,

00:02:55.178 --> 00:02:56.328
that's what you need to do.

00:02:56.328 --> 00:02:59.326
And then, the more gigs you
do, the better you'll get.

00:02:59.326 --> 00:03:00.490
Now and then you'll have a (bleep) gig,

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:01.939
but that's all right, 'cuz
you've got it out of you

00:03:01.939 --> 00:03:03.523
and you've experienced it.

00:03:03.523 --> 00:03:05.050
So I'd just say, the
more and more you can do.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:06.781
Put in your 10,000 hours,

00:03:06.781 --> 00:03:08.347
write as many songs as possible,

00:03:08.347 --> 00:03:09.180
gig as much as possible.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.211
What I've learned about music today is

00:03:11.211 --> 00:03:14.085
no one has to buy your record anymore.

00:03:14.085 --> 00:03:15.832
No one has to.

00:03:15.832 --> 00:03:17.101
Usually they will buy your record

00:03:17.101 --> 00:03:18.095
if you're a likable person.

00:03:18.095 --> 00:03:19.162
Adele is incredibly likable.

00:03:19.162 --> 00:03:20.320
Taylor's incredibly likable.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:23.533
Bruno Mars is incredibly
likable, Sam smith is likable.

00:03:23.533 --> 00:03:24.747
I'd like to think that I was likable.

00:03:24.747 --> 00:03:26.372
You have to be a likable person

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:27.893
- [Charlie] That's a huge
hit list you just named.

00:03:27.893 --> 00:03:31.106
- But for people to be likable persons,

00:03:31.106 --> 00:03:32.620
you have to get to know them.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.564
On Twitter, it's very
much instant thoughts.

00:03:36.564 --> 00:03:38.031
Snapchat's very much instant thoughts.

00:03:38.031 --> 00:03:39.498
Facebooks instant, instant, instant.

00:03:39.498 --> 00:03:42.702
It's all like your personality
coming out through something.

00:03:42.702 --> 00:03:45.952
You'd find kids, instead of being like,

00:03:46.804 --> 00:03:48.339
oh, I'm going to illegally
download the album

00:03:48.339 --> 00:03:49.172
'cuz I want to hear it.

00:03:49.172 --> 00:03:51.138
They'll be like, oh I
think they're a nice person

00:03:51.138 --> 00:03:52.067
I'm going to support that,

00:03:52.067 --> 00:03:54.072
and then they'll go and buy the album.

00:03:54.072 --> 00:03:58.071
I think that's a really
important thing nowadays.

00:03:58.071 --> 00:03:59.452
I've never been disproven.

00:03:59.452 --> 00:04:01.510
'Cuz anyone that comes across as a dick

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:02.875
does not sell that many records.

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:05.136
- You think somehow that
aspect of your personality

00:04:05.136 --> 00:04:07.594
will come out and turn off people?

00:04:07.594 --> 00:04:09.247
- I honestly do, I honestly do.

00:04:09.247 --> 00:04:13.414
I think, it's a lot different
then back in the day.

00:04:14.997 --> 00:04:17.769
If I was a successful
musician in the seventies,

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:19.488
and there was a newspaper
article about me,

00:04:19.488 --> 00:04:20.716
it could be true, it could not be true.

00:04:20.716 --> 00:04:24.542
There's TMZ footage now,
on line, on YouTube.

00:04:24.542 --> 00:04:28.355
Like it's there, it definitely happened.

00:04:28.355 --> 00:04:30.941
I think it's very important
to be a good person,

00:04:30.941 --> 00:04:32.249
nowadays in music.

00:04:32.249 --> 00:04:35.203
I found that everyone at a certain level,

00:04:35.203 --> 00:04:36.263
they're all nice, everyone's nice.

00:04:36.263 --> 00:04:39.263
Everyone who's mid-tier, who's very,

00:04:40.387 --> 00:04:41.720
what's the word?

00:04:42.866 --> 00:04:45.418
Self conscious about
themselves and their talent,

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:47.565
they're the ones who get a bit nasty.

00:04:47.565 --> 00:04:49.027
The biggest lesson I've learned from like

00:04:49.027 --> 00:04:53.248
breakthrough success is
everything is not what it seems.

00:04:53.248 --> 00:04:55.551
From good things to bad things,

00:04:55.551 --> 00:04:58.801
everything has a kind of silver lining.

00:04:59.755 --> 00:05:03.013
I think it's a healthy way to live

00:05:03.013 --> 00:05:05.736
when you're in this industry,
to be a little bit cautious.

00:05:05.736 --> 00:05:08.056
'Cuz I came into this industry with

00:05:08.056 --> 00:05:10.546
very, very wide eyes, and
like everything is great.

00:05:10.546 --> 00:05:11.379
Everyone is my friend.

00:05:11.379 --> 00:05:13.245
Then you kind of realize the more,

00:05:13.245 --> 00:05:16.654
the deeper you get into it, it
kind of shrinks a little bit.

00:05:16.654 --> 00:05:17.705
My dad always said to me,

00:05:17.705 --> 00:05:21.577
if you don't need to get
a degree to do a job,

00:05:21.577 --> 00:05:24.026
then go out and experience it.

00:05:24.026 --> 00:05:25.952
Like, if you want to be a doctor

00:05:25.952 --> 00:05:27.060
go and get your grades and stuff,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.341
but if you want to be a musician,

00:05:28.341 --> 00:05:31.019
your education is going
to come from doing shows,

00:05:31.019 --> 00:05:33.896
writing songs, practicing,
meeting people, networking.

00:05:33.896 --> 00:05:37.118
So just start off as early
as possible and do that.

00:05:37.118 --> 00:05:40.118
I've been influenced by many people.

00:05:41.253 --> 00:05:44.407
I've watched a Coldplay DVD numerous times

00:05:44.407 --> 00:05:47.105
and taken little tricks that Chris Martin

00:05:47.105 --> 00:05:48.322
does with the crowd.

00:05:48.322 --> 00:05:52.085
Even if it's just like,
sing this part here.

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:55.252
One thing I learned quite early on is,

00:05:57.406 --> 00:06:00.106
my friend went to go see U2.

00:06:00.106 --> 00:06:01.557
I think they were singing
Streets Have No Name,

00:06:01.557 --> 00:06:02.390
or something.

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:05.099
He'd paid money to be in the
front row, and he was there.

00:06:05.099 --> 00:06:07.497
And then Bono goes, about
to get to the chorus,

00:06:07.497 --> 00:06:08.841
and Bono goes, now you sing.

00:06:08.841 --> 00:06:12.536
And my friend was like,
no, no, no you sing

00:06:12.536 --> 00:06:13.645
I paid to come and see you.

00:06:13.645 --> 00:06:15.919
So now, every time when I play,

00:06:15.919 --> 00:06:17.793
I'll make sure I do the whole song,

00:06:17.793 --> 00:06:19.272
and then the singalong happens.

00:06:19.272 --> 00:06:22.889
So I'll make sure people
get what they paid for,

00:06:22.889 --> 00:06:25.282
and then, 'cuz having
the crowd sing it back

00:06:25.282 --> 00:06:26.413
is the most amazing thing in the world.

00:06:26.413 --> 00:06:28.401
I kind of wanted to have
that for the whole concert,

00:06:28.401 --> 00:06:30.319
but then also you need to,

00:06:30.319 --> 00:06:32.926
so I give as much as I can,
and then you do the singalong.

00:06:32.926 --> 00:06:35.584
Basically I was an actor
before I was a musician.

00:06:35.584 --> 00:06:37.677
- You look like an
actor, when you said that

00:06:37.677 --> 00:06:38.510
Did you see that?

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:41.904
You actually went, basically
I was an actor before I was,

00:06:41.904 --> 00:06:42.737
let me tell you.

00:06:42.737 --> 00:06:45.496
That was my main thing, I loved acting.

00:06:45.496 --> 00:06:46.984
I was in loads of theater groups.

00:06:46.984 --> 00:06:49.041
I did that at school all the time.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:51.004
And then I quickly realized, like,

00:06:51.004 --> 00:06:53.962
the weird thing is, I can
show you in 30 seconds

00:06:53.962 --> 00:06:55.530
how talented I am at music,

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:56.941
but I can't show you in 30 seconds

00:06:56.941 --> 00:06:59.784
how talented I am at acting.

00:06:59.784 --> 00:07:00.874
I realized, like,

00:07:00.874 --> 00:07:03.453
I can't actually have a
successful acting career

00:07:03.453 --> 00:07:05.447
without having a good part first.

00:07:05.447 --> 00:07:07.780
I just don't like auditions.

00:07:09.594 --> 00:07:11.801
For me, there was a point where

00:07:11.801 --> 00:07:14.710
I auditioned for this I-tv show,

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:17.440
and it was music and acting.

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.312
I thought, oh this will be fun.

00:07:19.312 --> 00:07:21.610
I got down to the final six.

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:23.306
I said to myself, if I get this,

00:07:23.306 --> 00:07:24.574
no more music, just acting.

00:07:24.574 --> 00:07:26.370
And if I don't, no more
acting, just music.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:27.465
And, I didn't.

00:07:27.465 --> 00:07:28.677
And then just did music.

00:07:28.677 --> 00:07:29.904
Then it kind of all went well.

00:07:29.904 --> 00:07:31.181
But, I've always wanted to act.

00:07:31.181 --> 00:07:34.010
But I didn't want to do
something for the sake of it.

00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:34.976
I'm going to play you a song.

00:07:34.976 --> 00:07:36.197
So this is my thing,

00:07:36.197 --> 00:07:39.364
when people go, oh you're so talented.

00:07:40.285 --> 00:07:41.616
You were born with natural talent.

00:07:41.616 --> 00:07:43.324
I'm like, hmm, nope.

00:07:43.324 --> 00:07:44.577
(audience laughs)

00:07:44.577 --> 00:07:45.902
Have a listen to this, right.

00:07:45.902 --> 00:07:47.738
This is the um--

00:07:47.738 --> 00:07:49.672
- Has this been played in public before?

00:07:49.672 --> 00:07:50.919
- No
- Okay

00:07:50.919 --> 00:07:53.117
- So this is um
- This is an exclusive.

00:07:53.117 --> 00:07:54.530
Ed Sheeran when he was (bleep) basically.

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:56.052
- Basically, all I'm saying is,

00:07:56.052 --> 00:07:59.712
when people say artist's are
born with talent, you're not.

00:07:59.712 --> 00:08:01.883
You have to really learn
and really practice.

00:08:01.883 --> 00:08:03.524
This me playing guitar and singing.

00:08:03.524 --> 00:08:04.853
I'm going to put it into my mic like this.

00:08:04.853 --> 00:08:06.936
(guitar)

00:08:10.287 --> 00:08:12.465
- It's okay so far.

00:08:12.465 --> 00:08:15.298
- It starts good, I had the strum.

00:08:17.563 --> 00:08:18.396
Hold me.

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:23.471
♫ I'm addicted to you

00:08:23.471 --> 00:08:26.304
(audience laughs)

00:08:28.073 --> 00:08:30.684
♫ But I'm good (voice cracks)

00:08:30.684 --> 00:08:32.023
- Oh, oh.

00:08:32.023 --> 00:08:34.273
(applause)

00:08:36.182 --> 00:08:39.283
- But you know what, you know what,

00:08:39.283 --> 00:08:40.533
I did practice.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.770
- After that you practiced?

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:46.000
- I probably needed it.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.417
- So thinking out loud 720 million views.

00:08:51.469 --> 00:08:53.886
720 million views on YouTube.

00:08:55.269 --> 00:08:57.019
How does that happen?

00:08:58.244 --> 00:09:00.117
- That was a conscious decision actually.

00:09:00.117 --> 00:09:02.867
There's certain things you can do

00:09:04.074 --> 00:09:05.802
to make a music video go viral,

00:09:05.802 --> 00:09:08.805
which is quite a difficult
thing to do nowadays.

00:09:08.805 --> 00:09:09.856
So you just have to do something

00:09:09.856 --> 00:09:12.040
that makes someone click
on a video, watch it,

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:13.609
and go I want to share
that with 10 people.

00:09:13.609 --> 00:09:15.576
I have to share that with 10 people.

00:09:15.576 --> 00:09:17.967
I never appear in my music videos.

00:09:17.967 --> 00:09:20.714
I always make a like
a little little cameo,

00:09:20.714 --> 00:09:23.050
if I appear at all.

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:25.700
I'm not a very svelte person.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:29.825
I'm not very talented at
much else other than music.

00:09:29.825 --> 00:09:30.658
But I decided--

00:09:30.658 --> 00:09:31.854
- [Charlie] It doesn't seem
to have gotten in the way.

00:09:31.854 --> 00:09:34.899
- Well, but I wanted to make a music video

00:09:34.899 --> 00:09:36.390
where I did a ballroom dance.

00:09:36.390 --> 00:09:40.557
A la So You Think You can
Dance, but not on tv reality.

00:09:41.987 --> 00:09:44.726
I trained on tour five hours a day,

00:09:44.726 --> 00:09:46.069
to learn this ballroom dance.

00:09:46.069 --> 00:09:47.666
Shot the music video, in a suit.

00:09:47.666 --> 00:09:48.672
I never wear suits either.

00:09:48.672 --> 00:09:50.612
It was basically doing the opposite of

00:09:50.612 --> 00:09:53.370
what anyone thought I was as an artist.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:55.450
And then seeing it, and
then everyone was like

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:57.169
oh, that's weird.

00:09:57.169 --> 00:09:59.266
And then they shared it
with lots of other people,

00:09:59.266 --> 00:10:00.796
and then it just kind of blew up.

00:10:00.796 --> 00:10:01.780
But the reason I chose it for that song,

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:03.837
is I knew that song was the
biggest song on the album.

00:10:03.837 --> 00:10:06.714
To do the most standout
video for the biggest song,

00:10:06.714 --> 00:10:09.764
when you combine them it both blows up.

00:10:09.764 --> 00:10:11.008
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:10:11.008 --> 00:10:14.023
